20:28:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: You’re absolutely right: when you look at a red apple, white light (from the sun or a lamp) hits it, the apple absorbs most wavelengths except red, which bounces off and reaches your eyes. That’s called **subtractive color mixing**—the object subtracts (absorbs) part of the light. A computer or TV screen works differently because it **emits its own light** directly. There’s no outside light bouncing off it. Screens use **additive color mixing**, where colors are created by adding different amounts of red, green, and blue light together—the three primary colors of light. ### How the screen physically does it Every pixel on an LCD, OLED, or LED screen is actually made up of three tiny subpixels: one **red**, one **green**, and one **blue** (often called RGB). These subpixels are so small and close together that your eye can’t separate them; you only see the combined color. - To make **yellow**, the screen turns on the red and green subpixels at full brightness, and the blue stays off. Your eye blends red + green light into yellow. - **Cyan** = green + blue. - **Magenta** = red + blue. - **White** = all three on at full. - **Black** = all off (no light emitted), though on many screens it’s just the dark background showing through. - Shades and tints are made by varying the *intensity* of each subpixel (for example, dim red + full green = a lime green; half red + half green = a darker yellow-brown). ### Why red, green, and blue? Because the human eye has three types of color-sensitive cone cells, roughly tuned to red, green, and blue light. By mixing those three, you can stimulate the cones in patterns that recreate almost any color in the visible spectrum—at least well enough for our brains to perceive a full range. So in summary: - **Reflective surfaces** (paint, apples) work by **subtracting** parts of white light. - **Screens** work by **adding** red, green, and blue light directly to your eyes, blending them in tiny subpixels. It’s like having millions of microscopic red, green, and blue light bulbs that mix in just the right way to paint an image.
